`src/limit.rs:3`\n\n```rust\n(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n```\n\n`0..=MAX_LINES` yields `0,1,2,3` — four values for a constant named `MAX_LINES = 3`. The function therefore returns 4 lines, not 3, contradicting its name, the constant, and the `MAX_LINES` contract. No input can ever produce exactly `MAX_LINES` correct output: slices of length exactly 4 succeed but return 4 elements.\n\nConcrete failing input: `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"])` returns `[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]` (length 4 > `MAX_LINES`).\n\n**Smallest fix:** change the range to exclusive, `0..MAX_LINES`.\n\n### 2. High — Reachable panic on valid input: index out of bounds\n**File:** `src/limit.rs:3`\n\n```rust\n(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n```\n\nUnconditional `lines[i]` indexing with a range whose length (4) is fixed and independent of `lines.len()`. Any slice shorter than 4 elements panics before the `collect` can do anything useful. Empty and short slices are ordinary, valid `&[&str]` values, so this is a reachable panic on valid input, not just on misuse.\n\nConcrete failing inputs:\n- `first_lines(&[])` → panics `index out of bounds: the len is 0 but the index is 0`\n- `first_lines(&[\"a\"])` → panics with `index 1, len is 1`\n- `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])` → panics `index out of bounds: the len is 3 but the index is 3` — this is the exact-input case the constant implies should succeed, which makes the bug user-visible rather than defensive-indexing.\n\n**Smallest fix:** take from the iterator over the slice instead of indexing:\n\n```rust\nlines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()\n```\n\nThis fixes #1 and #2 together, keeps the panic out of the function, and clamps short slices to what exists. If the intent is instead *hard failure* on short input, that should be an explicit `Result`, not an implicit panic.\n\n## Notes on correctness of the remaining surface\nNo integer-type mismatch (`MAX_LINES` is `usize`, matching slice indexing), no overflow risk (`RangeInclusive` does not compute `MAX_LINES + 1`, and the value is 3), no re-entrancy or race surface (pure function, no shared state), and the conversion `&str -> String` loses no data.\n\n## Coverage limits\n- `src/limit.rs` has **no callers and no tests** anywhere in the workspace — a workspace-wide search for `first_lines`, `MAX_LINES`, and `limit::` matched only the definition file itself, and there is no `main.rs`/`lib.rs`/`tests/`. I could not confirm the intended contract (truncate vs. require `len >= MAX_LINES` vs. error) from any call site; fix #2 assumes truncation, which is the conventional reading of `first_lines`.\n- `store/v1/` contains only runtime data (`sessions/*.json`, `wal/session-store.ndjson`, `wal/session-store.lock`) with no Rust source, so it imposes no constraint on this function.\n- `code_diagnostics` returned `rust-analyzer reported warning health`, so I could not obtain compiler/clippy confirmation; the out-of-bounds analysis is from the language semantics of slice indexing. A compile is not needed to confirm either finding, but a `cargo test` with the three inputs listed above would reproduce finding #2 immediately.", exit_code: 0, metadata: Some(Object {"task_id": String("task-35189d9a-9740-46e8-8f6e-43d01ca0fae2"), "session_id": String("task-run-task-35189d9a-9740-46e8-8f6e-43d01ca0fae2"), "agent": String("review"), "success": Bool(true), "output_bytes": Number(3417), "truncated_for_context": Bool(false), "artifact_id": String("task-output:task-35189d9a-9740-46e8-8f6e-43d01ca0fae2"), "artifact_uri": String("aster://tasks/task-run-task-35189d9a-9740-46e8-8f6e-43d01ca0fae2/runs/task-35189d9a-9740-46e8-8f6e-43d01ca0fae2/output"), "structured": Null, "source_anchors": Array [Object {"tool": String("read"), "url_or_path": String("src/limit.rs")}, Object {"tool": String("search"), "url_or_path": String("src/limit.rs")}], "aster_tool_result_evidence": Object {"schema": String("aster.code.tool